;; The tree registry.
;;
;; A symbol's tree is where it is written, never what it is spelled like.
;; CONFIG_ is not a namespace: Linux, BusyBox and U-Boot all use it for three
;; unrelated symbol spaces, and Buildroot builds its own conf with the prefix
;; set to the empty string. So every tree other than the two built in
;; (buildroot and linux) is declared here, and a fragment configures one with
;; (scope NAME ...).
;;
;; consumed-by is the Buildroot symbol that hands the tree's emitted config
;; fragment to its build. Each was checked to exist, as a string, in Buildroot
;; 2025.02.16; `silt check --buildroot` re-checks them against whatever tree
;; it is given. prefix is a spelling check only.
;;
;; No Kconfig tree for these is imported yet, so their symbols are carried and
;; emitted but not verified. Devicetree is deliberately absent: it binds to
;; drivers by compatible string at runtime and is not a constraint system.
;;
;; linux is built in, and `silt check --linux DIR` verifies every CONFIG_*
;; claim against a kernel checkout — the Kconfig files alone are enough. A
;; (source "...") here would point at a fixed path on one machine, so the path
;; stays on the command line. ARCH defaults to arm64; override it with
;; (tree linux (kind kconfig) (env (ARCH "x86_64"))) in a library that targets
;; something else.

(tree busybox
  (kind kconfig)
  (prefix "CONFIG_")
  (consumed-by BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES))

(tree uboot
  (kind kconfig)
  (prefix "CONFIG_")
  (consumed-by BR2_TARGET_UBOOT_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES))

;; uClibc-ng spells most symbols UCLIBC_, but not all (ARCH_*, DO_C99_MATH), so
;; no prefix is claimed.
(tree uclibc
  (kind kconfig)
  (consumed-by BR2_UCLIBC_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES))

;; The other half of an appliance.
;;
;; A gateway on a Linux board and a microcontroller on a wall are two
;; computers, configured by two tools, in two files - and the things they must
;; agree about are agreed by hand: the topic one publishes and the other
;; subscribes to, the address one has and the route the other advertises,
;; whether they speak MQTT or ESPHome's native API. Each is one fact written
;; twice with nothing checking the copies, and a pair that builds, flashes and
;; boots perfectly on both sides can be unable to talk.
;;
;; Silt does not build the firmware; esphome does, the way Buildroot builds
;; images. And it does not model a configuration language that gains components
;; every month. It models the interface - the surface where two configurations
;; have to agree - because that is where the mistakes are.
;;
;; Settings are dotted paths rather than Kconfig symbols: mqtt.topic_prefix,
;; switch.toggle.pin. The constraint model is unchanged. Two fragments setting
;; one path to different values is a conflict, and an image resolves it with
;; (override ...) exactly as anywhere else.
(tree esphome
  (kind esphome))
